<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mahé de La Bourdonnais</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1699–1753</span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Bertrand-François Mahé de La Bourdonnais</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bertrand-François Mahé de La Bourdonnais was a French naval officer who governed Isle de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and Île <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-bourbon">Bourbon</a> for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-east-india-company">French East India Company</a> from 1735 to 1746. He built <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/port-louis">Port Louis</a> as a capital and naval base and developed plantations worked by enslaved people. In 1746 he captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chennai">Madras</a> from the British, but a quarrel with Dupleix over its ransom led to his imprisonment in the Bastille.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mahe-de-la-bourdonnais.jpg" alt="Mahé de La Bourdonnais" width="320" height="386" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Antoine Graincourt · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bertrand_Fran%C3%A7ois_Mah%C3%A9_de_La_Bourdonnais.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
